WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.854
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.854 --> 00:00:07.089
Once again, thank you so much for
being here with us today.

00:00:07.089 --> 00:00:08.160
We really appreciate it.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.200
&gt;&gt; I'm thrilled to be here.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.231
&gt;&gt; As Sarah alluded to in your
introduction, you had an upbringing and

00:00:14.231 --> 00:00:18.898
sought to your career as a death penalty
lawyer that was very different to most of

00:00:18.898 --> 00:00:21.351
your compatriots in the Fortune 500.

00:00:21.351 --> 00:00:23.463
It's a little bit more
about how that upbringing,

00:00:23.463 --> 00:00:25.780
that experience in your early
career shaped you as man?

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:29.590
&gt;&gt; So let me just start by saying again,
I'm thrilled to be here.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:31.050
It's a wonderful place to be.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.050
I think what my upbringing really taught
me, because I was born on the other side

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:43.190
of the tracks and
had the opportunity because social

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:47.880
engineers engaged in what was
then called school desegregation.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:52.310
I was raised in the inner city of
Philadelphia, had many older siblings.

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:53.360
But my younger sister and

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.520
I were born at a time when
experimentation was happening in America.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:01.830
And one of the experiments was,
it was called school desegregation,

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:05.650
which is different from integration
in that the social engineers in

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:09.580
Philadelphia felt that a few inner city
black children should be put on buses for

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:14.020
90 minutes one way to go to
the best schools in Philadelphia.

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:17.800
As a result of that,
which by the way I hated as a child,

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.680
I realize now looking back,
that I was given an opportunity

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:27.010
that most of my friends and
even my siblings were not given.

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:30.820
And so I think what that caused
me to realized is that there is,

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:34.758
in fact, this opportunity gap
that exist in our society.

00:01:34.758 --> 00:01:37.690
That if you're lucky
to be put on a bus for

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:40.740
90 minutes a day,
it closes that gap for years.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:44.320
But there are lots of other
people who are similarly talented

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.580
who don't get those opportunities.

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:50.810
So I started off, I guess,
in my career, thinking about how do

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:54.560
those of us who are lucky enough to have
these opportunities, how do we get back,

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.740
how do we figure out how we can help
those other people who were not so

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.490
fortunate, not so privileged,
to actually reach their full potential.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.112
&gt;&gt; You were a lawyer for a long time and

00:02:06.112 --> 00:02:06.822
you won-
&gt;&gt; But

00:02:06.822 --> 00:02:08.845
not in a perjorative sense of the word,
okay?

00:02:08.845 --> 00:02:13.530
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You were a business lawyer and then

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:17.410
now you're the CEO of one of the biggest
pharmaceutical companies in the world.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:18.070
How does that happen?

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.370
How does a lawyer get to that stage?

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:23.220
&gt;&gt; So the first thing I have
to say about career paths, and

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.088
I've had this conversation
with a lowt of people,

00:02:25.088 --> 00:02:28.970
is that there's an element of
serendipity in everybody's career path.

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:35.300
So I never aspired to be
a pharmaceutical executive.

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:38.920
As you mentioned, and I had
the opportunity in the private practice of

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:41.700
law to do things that I thought
were really be great adventures,

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.580
whether it's teaching in South Africa,
doing death penalty work.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:49.830
But one day, the phone rang.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:53.870
I answered the phone and
it was the General Counsel of Merck,

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:57.530
who said I'd like you to come and
meet with me and the then CEO of Merck,

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:03.730
a really great CEO named Roy Vagelos,
one of the great CEOs of recent history.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:08.580
I want you to come and interview with us
because we'd like to bring you to Merck.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:11.150
And I said, well, okay,
I'll come interview.

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:15.180
And I went home that night and
I said to my wonderful spouse, Andre,

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.970
who's in the front room.

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:21.160
That I intended to humor my client by
going for an interview, and she said,

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:24.850
honey, I don't want to tell you about your
business, but this whole law firm thing is

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:28.480
a cruel hoax and you might want to
think more openly about it.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.410
So I did what she told me to do.

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:35.019
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And I ended up going to Merck, a little

00:03:35.019 --> 00:03:39.161
bit against my will because I didn't
think it would be nearly as adventurous,

00:03:39.161 --> 00:03:43.303
being in a corporation as being in a firm
where you could represent anybody that

00:03:43.303 --> 00:03:44.030
you wanted.

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:47.530
But little did I know that there was
going to be a real as I worked there.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:50.910
&gt;&gt; Through serendipity or not,

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.320
a lot of us ended up here
[INAUDIBLE] in the world of MBAs.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:54.898
In the policy,

00:03:54.898 --> 00:03:57.659
you've been quoted as being slightly
skeptical with the MBA as a degree.

00:03:57.659 --> 00:04:00.135
I believe the exact words were I'm
not sure Bill Gates ever had one.

00:04:00.135 --> 00:04:04.244
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Don't worry, we get a lot.

00:04:04.244 --> 00:04:07.170
But I'm curious,
what is it you think we're missing?

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:10.440
What is it you think we need to go out and
learn in the next phase of our careers?

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:15.270
&gt;&gt; So
let me say that I think business education

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:18.740
really has its values, so you should
be encouraged to hear me say that.

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:24.840
But I think the issues that we face in
business are broader than the traditional

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:29.730
issues around finance, around marketing,

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.850
I think the issues that
we have to address is,

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:38.130
as businesses are generally
the issues that society faces.

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:41.827
I believe that the biggest problems
that we have in society can only be

00:04:41.827 --> 00:04:47.120
addressed through sustainable means, and I
think businesses can address those issues.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.850
But only if their leaders are willing
to take a broad point of view about

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:53.560
what it is that the business exist to do?

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:56.800
What is the fundamental
purpose of the business?

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:59.530
We have this discussion now,
and unfortunately,

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:03.950
I think what people like Larry think it's
become more of a widespread discussion

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:08.160
about whether corporations exist solely
to create value for their shareholders or

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.840
whether they have a broader
purpose in the world.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:15.148
I happen to believe very strongly that
a company like Merck has a real broad,

00:05:15.148 --> 00:05:17.788
significant, salient purpose in the world.

00:05:17.788 --> 00:05:22.876
It's 127 years old and the reason why
it's lasted more than a century is

00:05:22.876 --> 00:05:27.822
that its purpose goes beyond simply
making money for its shareholders.

00:05:27.822 --> 00:05:30.847
If you get up in the morning and
your sole intent is to make money for

00:05:30.847 --> 00:05:34.752
your shareholders, you'll miss some really
great opportunities to do a lot of good

00:05:34.752 --> 00:05:35.690
for a lot of people.

00:05:36.972 --> 00:05:39.810
&gt;&gt; That must particularly hard for
a company like Merck where you've got

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:43.380
employees, you've got shareholders,
and most of all, you've got patients.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:46.190
How do you go about weighing up
those different constituents

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:48.080
whenever you make a decision?

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:51.450
&gt;&gt; Well, there's no formulaic
way to do it, obviously.

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:54.500
But I think the first thing
is that you have to remember

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:56.978
that the company exists for this purpose.

00:05:56.978 --> 00:06:00.670
So I came to work for
a really great CEO named Roy Vagelos.

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:03.560
And I could tell you a lot of stories,
but the thing that I remember more than

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:07.300
anything else is he used to say to me and
he still says to me.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:10.392
He's 89 years old, and
I still see him frequently.

00:06:10.392 --> 00:06:15.280
He says, there are a lot of metrics
around on the way the company operates.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.070
But just remember at Merck,
there are two fundamental metrics,

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:22.790
that if you remember as CEO, you can go
through all your operating reviews, and

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:26.360
you can look at your margins, and
you can look at your cost of goods sold,

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:29.150
you can look at all these
wonderful metrics, but

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:31.870
there's only two metrics
in the end that matters.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:34.920
Number one, how many people do you help?

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:40.340
And number two,
how much help do you give those people?

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:43.910
And if you're maximizing
the number of people you help, and

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:47.240
you're maximizing the amount of
good that you do for the people

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:51.000
that you are helping, then all of those
other metrics will fall into place.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:56.430
&gt;&gt; I'd love to unpack that some
more later on in the interview,

00:06:56.430 --> 00:07:00.020
bur firstly, there are a few
times when I was researching for

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.800
this interview that I came across
times when you had to make really,

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:05.850
really difficult choices,
made very brave choices.

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.390
For example,
taking the James Cochran case,

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:10.730
leaving the President's
Manufacturing Council.

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:15.090
You also took on highly sensitive internal
investigation into the Paterno Sandusky

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:18.910
case at Penn State, and I hugely admired
how you held your ground against some

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:22.280
people who were trying to sweep
certain elements under the rug.

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:25.260
How do you know when to act when other
people around you are being silent or

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:26.785
indecisive?

00:07:26.785 --> 00:07:29.900
&gt;&gt; Well, I had great parents.

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:32.610
But my mother died when I was young,
so I had the experience of

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:36.550
being raised by a single parent
father who was like a drill sergeant.

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:39.000
He was a very unsentimental person.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:40.890
But he said to me when I was very young,

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:44.990
probably the best piece of advice that
I've ever gotten from anybody else.

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:46.160
And here it is in a nutshell.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.110
He used to say to me, Kenny,

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:52.535
what other people think about you
is none of your damn business.

00:07:52.535 --> 00:07:55.330
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:59.170
when I was young I would think,
how could that be?

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:06.370
I want to be Liked, I want to be friendly
with all my colleagues around me.

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:10.150
I want people to like me and
enjoy being around me.

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:15.500
But the more I've experienced in life,
I understand exactly what he was saying.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:21.130
He was saying that if you can,
focus on what's the right thing to do.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:25.570
Irrespective of whether people
around you like you or not.

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:28.430
Then you can do the right thing,
so every one of those situations,

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:32.560
the Sandusky investigation was
a very difficult investigation.

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:34.640
I'm a very proud alum of Penn State.

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:42.290
The week before the Sandusky grand jury,
I actually exchanged correspondence

00:08:42.290 --> 00:08:45.990
with Joe Paterno who, there'll never
be another coach like Joe Paterno.

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:49.307
No one will ever coach at this school for
60 years.

00:08:49.307 --> 00:08:51.000
He was a great man.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:53.220
He did a lot of wonderful things.

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:56.469
But when we began to see some of
the things that were happening behind

00:08:56.469 --> 00:08:57.552
the scenes as a board,

00:08:57.552 --> 00:09:00.858
then we had to decide whether we were
going to put the children first,

00:09:00.858 --> 00:09:04.403
or whether we we were going to put
the reputation of the university first.

00:09:04.403 --> 00:09:06.960
And we chose to do what we
thought was the right thing.

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:08.942
I can't say that everybody agrees with us.

00:09:08.942 --> 00:09:12.722
In fact, many of our alums think we
should have represented the university.

00:09:12.722 --> 00:09:14.374
We should have defended the university.

00:09:14.374 --> 00:09:16.378
We should have defended
the football program.

00:09:16.378 --> 00:09:20.998
And I think they have a legitimate
point of view from where they sit.

00:09:20.998 --> 00:09:25.276
And the end of the day though, it seemed
to me that what was right in the long

00:09:25.276 --> 00:09:29.488
run was that we took a strong stand for
what needs to happen for children.

00:09:29.488 --> 00:09:30.843
And you see it happening everywhere.

00:09:30.843 --> 00:09:33.760
You see it happening at
Michigan State now, right?

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:35.529
Where the same kinds of things went on.

00:09:35.529 --> 00:09:39.093
So I think whether it's Penn State,
you take a death penalty case,

00:09:39.093 --> 00:09:41.100
it sounds great in retrospect.

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:45.600
When you start that case, my client
was 13 days before his execution day.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.593
There was a lot of hostility
in that part of Alabama,

00:09:48.593 --> 00:09:53.330
because people had bought in to the story
that the conviction was correct.

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:57.545
And in comes this northern lawyer, by the
way, wearing a blue suit, which you're not

00:09:57.545 --> 00:10:01.380
supposed to wear in that part of Alabama,
because we're still fighting that war.

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:02.343
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:10:02.343 --> 00:10:07.548
I'm here to say that the community's
perspective, that the community's

00:10:07.548 --> 00:10:11.709
story about what happened in
the night in question is wrong.

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:16.627
That creates a great deal of
anger in the people around you.

00:10:16.627 --> 00:10:20.230
And the only way in which you can do that
on a consistent basis is you really have

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:21.728
to believe what my father said,

00:10:21.728 --> 00:10:24.680
what they think about you ultimately
is none of your business.

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:28.732
&gt;&gt; You talked about doing the right thing.

00:10:28.732 --> 00:10:32.370
And I think a lot of people think we're
at a turning point society at the moment.

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:34.764
There's a real lost of
trust in institutions.

00:10:34.764 --> 00:10:36.920
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:40.100
&gt;&gt; And you mentioned earlier to us in the
greenroom that the kind of conflict you

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:41.440
see between capitalism and

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:45.870
democracy in this country and accepting
a certain manner of social inequality.

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:49.610
Is there a solution to this or is this
a endless series of decisions where

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:52.020
we have to rely on people to
make the right one every time?

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:56.030
&gt;&gt; Well,
I don't think that I can sit here and

00:10:56.030 --> 00:11:00.470
give you one short set of solutions.

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:05.815
What I would say is that what I am
greatly concerned about our society

00:11:05.815 --> 00:11:12.120
is the seeming inability for
us to have nuanced

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:16.760
constructive conversations about
the issues that exist in our society.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:18.220
And we will never reach

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:23.220
constructive solutions unless
we can have those conversations.

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:28.050
So, for example, frankly,
as I was growing up in

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:33.116
the 70s I would have viewed
myself as a socialist.

00:11:33.116 --> 00:11:36.775
The concept that I'm running a large
pharmaceutical company to me,

00:11:36.775 --> 00:11:39.900
sometimes I just can't believe it's true,
right?

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:42.690
I remember having an economics
professor in undergraduate school and

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:44.620
he was talking about capitalism.

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:47.486
He happened to be from Eastern Europe,
Professor Albinski.

00:11:47.486 --> 00:11:49.163
He was from Eastern Europe.

00:11:49.163 --> 00:11:51.214
And he was talking about
the virtues of a capitalist system.

00:11:51.214 --> 00:11:54.140
And I remember saying to him,
you're so conservative.

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:57.891
And he walked over and he leaned over, he
looked at me, he said, and you, sonny boy,

00:11:57.891 --> 00:11:59.265
have nothing yet to conserve.

00:11:59.265 --> 00:12:03.406
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I really get him now.

00:12:03.406 --> 00:12:06.350
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:09.820
here I am,
a staunch defender of the free market.

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:17.460
Well, the free market Inherently requires
some level of economic inequality.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:22.060
Those people who create value are going
to have more resources than those people

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:23.920
who consume value.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:28.130
We can't say that everybody is going to
have exactly the same amount of resources.

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:28.828
I accept that.

00:12:28.828 --> 00:12:32.760
I think it's actually the best
economic system in the world.

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:37.600
However, if you allow that economic
inequality to translate into

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:42.570
political inequality, for example,
people who have a lot of money can in fact

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:47.330
buy elected representatives,
then I have a problem with that.

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:50.590
And so how do we think about preserving

00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:54.640
the economic system in this country which
has created not only tremendous wealth for

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:58.280
people in this country but
around the world?

00:12:58.280 --> 00:13:00.106
While at the same time thinking,

00:13:00.106 --> 00:13:03.970
well, what's the right balance to have
in terms of our political system?

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:09.180
How much economic inequality are we
willing to tolerate in our country?

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:15.050
We now have a situation where our
economy creates a lot of value for

00:13:15.050 --> 00:13:16.440
people who hold capital.

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:21.790
Globalism has been great for
people who hold capital.

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:26.890
The technology revolution, which of
course is centered right out here in

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:31.510
Silicon Valley, it's created a lot of
value for people who hold capital.

00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:36.317
But if you're not well-educated,
if you're a person who has a labor job,

00:13:36.317 --> 00:13:39.112
all of those have actually been bad for
you.

00:13:39.112 --> 00:13:44.460
Because now machines take your place,
labor arbitrage takes your job away.

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:49.090
So how do we try to have a balanced
economy where everybody is

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:54.010
included without losing what's driven the
fundamental strength of our country for

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:56.860
years, which has been
the economic system that we have?

00:13:57.870 --> 00:13:59.400
I can't answer all those questions.

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:01.059
But I think, again,

00:14:01.059 --> 00:14:06.787
the point I'm making is it's not
a choice between one thing or the other.

00:14:06.787 --> 00:14:09.586
&gt;&gt; Certainly not asking you to
answer all those questions,

00:14:09.586 --> 00:14:12.250
but will ask you to answer
one particular one.

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:14.535
We're relying on decisive
leaders like you in healthcare.

00:14:14.535 --> 00:14:18.463
And we have a system that's hard for
many of us to be optimistic about.

00:14:18.463 --> 00:14:20.377
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You're already highly influential

00:14:20.377 --> 00:14:20.880
in the system.

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:22.720
If you could be king for the day,

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:25.380
what's the one thing you'd change about
the health care system in this country?

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:27.630
&gt;&gt; I would make it patient-centered.

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:33.920
I think the incentives in the system drive
people away from what's good for patients.

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:36.258
I'm a CEO of a pharmaceutical company.

00:14:36.258 --> 00:14:40.762
I think Merck goes great things in
the world, but when you sit down with our

00:14:40.762 --> 00:14:45.810
friends in the insurance business,
they have different economic incentives.

00:14:45.810 --> 00:14:48.445
Physicians have different
economic incentives.

00:14:48.445 --> 00:14:53.545
I think the only way in which we can
actually come together and kind of get

00:14:53.545 --> 00:14:58.815
alignment around the cost of the system,
around the value of the system,

00:14:58.815 --> 00:15:03.830
and the impact of the system on society
and our budgets in society, and

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:09.100
on patients, is to actually say,
what is good for patients as a whole?

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:12.190
Individually, and
from a population standpoint.

00:15:12.190 --> 00:15:16.222
I think if we would all agree that
we're here to serve patients and

00:15:16.222 --> 00:15:20.984
we want to do what's in the best interest
of patients, I think that would help

00:15:20.984 --> 00:15:25.333
us align the really badly aligned
incentives in our system right now.

00:15:25.333 --> 00:15:27.430
&gt;&gt; How does that kind
of conversation start?

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:28.790
How do ou get everyone
in the room together?

00:15:28.790 --> 00:15:29.880
Is it politically led?

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:31.040
Is it industry led?

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:32.720
&gt;&gt; We're trying do it.

00:15:32.720 --> 00:15:33.960
A bunch of us are going to try and

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:39.260
come together again in June from
across the entire healthcare spectrum.

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:42.328
We think of ourselves as
the enlightened ones.

00:15:42.328 --> 00:15:46.029
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We're all going to try and get together

00:15:46.029 --> 00:15:50.270
and see if we can have a conversation
that bridges some of those gaps.

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:55.720
I mean, if you listen to what was being
said the other day by President Trump and

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:59.840
Secretary Azar, a lot of what they
were focusing on is they can see

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:03.360
how badly misaligned
the incentives are in the system.

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:07.070
So, for example,
You hear a lot about drug pricing.

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:14.200
The system in which we actually sell drugs
in the commercial market is one in which,

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:19.760
if you actually lowered your list price,
you'd be less competitive.

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:22.510
As an antitrust lawyer,
I just can't get that through my head.

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:27.500
The way to be non-competitive in my
business is to lower your price.

00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:28.840
Why is that?

00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:32.360
Well, because many of
the actors in the supply chain

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:36.330
make money as a percentage
of the list price.

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:41.060
So for them high prices
are better than low prices.

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:41.990
Okay?

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:46.781
But if you're a person who's filling
a prescription at the pharmacy counter,

00:16:46.781 --> 00:16:49.770
you're paying a copay
based on a list price.

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:53.130
So you would actually
rather have a lower price.

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:56.830
We actually ran that experiment with
a hepatitis C drug that we came out with

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:57.830
a little while ago.

00:16:57.830 --> 00:17:02.130
We said, okay, we're the third
entrant in the market, this is a cure.

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:05.280
Let's see what happens if we bring this

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:08.380
product to market significantly
lower than our competition.

00:17:08.380 --> 00:17:10.520
Now, that was the category, hepatitis C,

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:15.800
where they used to talk about the $100,000
cure you might remember a few years ago.

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:19.030
We said let's see what happens if we
bring in a drug at a lower price.

00:17:19.030 --> 00:17:22.070
Well, I think the experiment
that we ran showed

00:17:22.070 --> 00:17:26.460
that a lot of actors in the system would
disfavor a lower list price product.

00:17:26.460 --> 00:17:29.070
And there's something wrong with
a system that operates that way.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:35.940
&gt;&gt; One of the other things in that speech
was referring to President Trump's notion,

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:39.450
I think shared by many people, that
foreign governments who run countries and

00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:42.280
public healthcare systems take
advantage of American consumers because

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:46.260
they're able to negotiate drugs in bulk,
effectively.

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:50.530
Is that notion correct, and
is there something that you can change?

00:17:50.530 --> 00:17:54.220
&gt;&gt; Well,
I think if you look across the world,

00:17:54.220 --> 00:17:58.190
I think it's pretty fair to say that
there's some countries in the world,

00:17:58.190 --> 00:18:03.750
particularly some of the higher
OECD countries in Western Europe,

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:09.790
That actually don't pay their fair share
of the cost of pharmaceutical research.

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:11.900
I think that's pretty clear.

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:14.680
How we change that,
I mean I just left London where I met with

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:18.140
some of the top health officials and
I have news for the President.

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:21.560
They're not actually interested
in paying more for our drugs.

00:18:21.560 --> 00:18:24.520
They're pretty clear about that.

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:28.612
They actually found some of those comments
mildly amusing, as they say in London.

00:18:28.612 --> 00:18:32.950
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] It's a real shocker they
didn't want to pay more for them.

00:18:32.950 --> 00:18:37.120
&gt;&gt; The problem is though, of course,

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:42.020
if Americans didn't, quote, overpay for
drugs, there would be no drugs.

00:18:42.020 --> 00:18:45.180
&gt;&gt; I mean,
that's the sad truth of the matter.

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:49.850
At the end of the day, the only thing
worse than Americans paying a very

00:18:49.850 --> 00:18:54.980
substantial amount for drugs, is Americans
not paying a substantial amount for drugs.

00:18:54.980 --> 00:18:56.880
Because if they didn't, frankly,

00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:00.970
we couldn't raise the capital to take
the risks that we do in R and D.

00:19:03.138 --> 00:19:04.770
&gt;&gt; You take risks in R and D for

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:09.540
some diseases that you're not
planning on making any money on.

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:14.660
You mentioned that today you shipped
Ebola vaccines free to the Congo.

00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:19.370
You, in the past, developed a cure for
River Valley Fever.

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:24.740
When is it you choose to go
after these particular diseases?

00:19:24.740 --> 00:19:27.073
How do you come up with that?

00:19:27.073 --> 00:19:31.826
&gt;&gt; So the way that we think about our
R and D enterprise at Merck is that

00:19:31.826 --> 00:19:35.977
we're pretty much agnostic
to therapeutic category and

00:19:35.977 --> 00:19:38.710
pretty much agnostic to modality.

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:43.030
That is to say, we don't say we
want to do a small molecule or

00:19:43.030 --> 00:19:44.970
a vaccine or a biologic.

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:48.280
And we don't say we want to do
this disease or that disease.

00:19:48.280 --> 00:19:51.230
We try to follow the scientific
opportunities that we get.

00:19:51.230 --> 00:19:55.480
And the reason that we ended up in Ebola
was the work that we were doing in our

00:19:55.480 --> 00:20:00.990
vaccine research organization really lent
itself to doing something about Ebola.

00:20:00.990 --> 00:20:05.050
At a time when frankly, we had that
last outbreak in western Africa,

00:20:05.050 --> 00:20:09.530
Sierra Leone, Guinea,
Liberia which killed 11,000 people and

00:20:09.530 --> 00:20:12.870
made 25,000 people sick before
we got it under control.

00:20:12.870 --> 00:20:15.840
We were able to look at some of
the work that had been done,

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:20.690
pioneering work done elsewhere, and
say the work that we know how to

00:20:20.690 --> 00:20:25.410
do inside Merck to sort of purify
the antigen to make sure that

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:30.150
we bring forward the right kinds of steps
to go from just having an antigen to

00:20:30.150 --> 00:20:34.800
actually having a full-fledged safe
vaccine is what we do every day.

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:38.825
We thought if we took that program over,
if we paid for that IP, if we brought it

00:20:38.825 --> 00:20:42.830
in-house, there was a good chance that
we could do something for the world.

00:20:42.830 --> 00:20:45.370
And I'm very proud that in
the field tests that drug,

00:20:45.370 --> 00:20:49.657
that vaccine has been shown to be,
so far, 100% effective.

00:20:51.081 --> 00:20:54.330
&gt;&gt; That's amazing.

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:56.480
And Merck does a lot more.

00:20:56.480 --> 00:21:03.729
&gt;&gt; Well,
it's hard to be more than 100% effective.

00:21:03.729 --> 00:21:07.940
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Merck does a lot more for
causes.

00:21:07.940 --> 00:21:11.260
And one cause I've heard you speak
about particularly passionately is

00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:13.200
preventing the death of
women in childbirth.

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:16.394
Why is this something you are so
passionate about or that Merck is so

00:21:16.394 --> 00:21:17.375
passionate about?

00:21:17.375 --> 00:21:22.035
&gt;&gt; So, I think, I don't want to
sound like I'm just a booster for

00:21:22.035 --> 00:21:26.786
Merck, but I will say that
the company has as part of its Ethos,

00:21:26.786 --> 00:21:31.490
the concept that we should
be contributing to humanity.

00:21:31.490 --> 00:21:35.100
And we don't just say, well,
we're contributing to humanity because

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:39.080
the medicines and vaccines that we
make actually have a positive impact.

00:21:39.080 --> 00:21:41.720
I have to say this industry
has a lot to be proud of.

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:45.100
At the beginning of the 20th century
global life expectancy was less than

00:21:45.100 --> 00:21:46.270
40 years.

00:21:46.270 --> 00:21:51.270
It's now above 70 years, largely
driven by the innovations by Merck and

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:52.550
other companies.

00:21:52.550 --> 00:21:57.330
But we don't say, because we invented
a measles vaccine which probably has saved

00:21:57.330 --> 00:22:01.960
20 million children's lives over the last
15 years, that that's all we need to do.

00:22:01.960 --> 00:22:05.140
We need to also look at those things
that we're not required to do.

00:22:05.140 --> 00:22:10.191
We need to find areas where we can bring
both our financial resources as well

00:22:10.191 --> 00:22:15.643
as our scientific expertise to bear and
we just chose maternal mortality because

00:22:15.643 --> 00:22:21.034
there's, 80% of women who die in child
birth die from preventable causes.

00:22:21.034 --> 00:22:25.865
And just to give you some data,
people think this is a problem in

00:22:25.865 --> 00:22:30.850
Sub-Saharan Africa or India or
other poor places in the world.

00:22:30.850 --> 00:22:35.435
In New York City, the chance that
a black woman dies in childbirth is 12

00:22:35.435 --> 00:22:39.960
times higher than the chance that
a white woman will die in childbirth.

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:41.780
12 times higher.

00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:45.868
If you look across all OECD countries,
in maternal mortality,

00:22:45.868 --> 00:22:48.600
the US ranks 32nd out of 35 countries.

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:53.570
I mean, that's an amazing statistic.

00:22:53.570 --> 00:22:56.070
So as we look at those things,
we know what to do.

00:22:56.070 --> 00:23:00.900
The problem in maternal mortality is
that we don't have these clear protocols

00:23:00.900 --> 00:23:05.800
to deal with the issues like hemorrhaging,
like spikes in blood pressure,

00:23:05.800 --> 00:23:07.200
like sepsis.

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:09.740
It used to be in this country that
if you had a heart attack, you died.

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:13.110
Now you don't die anymore because there
are clear protocols to what to do when

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:16.390
somebody presents at a hospital
with a heart attack.

00:23:16.390 --> 00:23:20.371
So we just decided that we would devote
a half a billion dollars over ten years

00:23:20.371 --> 00:23:24.180
to seeing how we could reduce
preventable cases of maternal mortality.

00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:26.640
And we feel good about that.

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:28.570
But there's also a business
benefit to that,

00:23:28.570 --> 00:23:34.620
because when we go to recruit world class
scientists that come out of academia,

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:40.600
those kinds of things are evidence to them
of what the company really cares about.

00:23:40.600 --> 00:23:43.490
And I think it's a tremendous
competitive advantage.

00:23:43.490 --> 00:23:47.310
When people can see the kind of impact
that we're having in areas like that.

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:52.066
&gt;&gt; You mentioned a truly horrifying
statistic, which is that black mothers

00:23:52.066 --> 00:23:54.722
are far more likely to die than
white mothers in [CROSSTALK].

00:23:54.722 --> 00:23:57.090
&gt;&gt; Yeah, only 12 times more.

00:23:57.090 --> 00:24:01.450
&gt;&gt; Is this an example of
institutionalized bias?

00:24:01.450 --> 00:24:04.800
What are the factors behind this and
what can be done about them?

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:09.750
So again, in the spirit of
having a nuanced conversation,

00:24:09.750 --> 00:24:14.950
I won't label it as just
the product of institutional bias,

00:24:14.950 --> 00:24:17.920
but clearly, there is institutional bias.

00:24:17.920 --> 00:24:22.090
Because the same studies that show
a black woman is 12 times more likely

00:24:22.090 --> 00:24:24.840
to die than a white woman
show that it almost

00:24:24.840 --> 00:24:28.430
doesn't matter what the socioeconomic
status of the black woman is.

00:24:28.430 --> 00:24:30.640
So, even when they have insurance,

00:24:30.640 --> 00:24:35.210
even when they present,
they don't get the same care.

00:24:35.210 --> 00:24:39.120
There's also the issue of what
happens in their environment, right?

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:43.730
And so, from my perspective,
that is an issue, I think in this case,

00:24:43.730 --> 00:24:49.360
because even if you control for
socioeconomic status.

00:24:49.360 --> 00:24:52.610
An African American woman in
New York City with an advanced degree

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:56.030
is more likely to die than a white
man without a high school degree.

00:24:56.030 --> 00:24:59.560
And I think it's pretty hard to square
those things without looking at

00:24:59.560 --> 00:25:02.950
how the healthcare system delivers
care to different people.

00:25:05.240 --> 00:25:08.165
&gt;&gt; There are clearly issues
more broadly in society, and

00:25:08.165 --> 00:25:11.098
we've all heard about
the opportunities gap before.

00:25:11.098 --> 00:25:13.397
You mentioned this earlier at
the beginning of the interview.

00:25:13.397 --> 00:25:17.230
In the past, I've heard you also mention
an access gap for minorities in business.

00:25:17.230 --> 00:25:19.340
Can you unpack this idea
a little further for us?

00:25:19.340 --> 00:25:22.210
&gt;&gt; Sure, when I talk about
the opportunity gap, I mean two things.

00:25:22.210 --> 00:25:23.940
And then I'll get to access gap.

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:28.480
So I talked about being bussed as part of
the experimentation when I was a young

00:25:28.480 --> 00:25:33.420
child in Philadelphia,
being bussed to majority schools,

00:25:33.420 --> 00:25:34.930
white schools,
let's call them what they were.

00:25:36.150 --> 00:25:37.936
So, what did that do for me?

00:25:37.936 --> 00:25:39.350
When I was in elementary,

00:25:39.350 --> 00:25:43.160
and middle, and high school,
I didn't think of myself as a student.

00:25:43.160 --> 00:25:48.110
I went to schools where the ambient
standard for education was very high.

00:25:48.110 --> 00:25:51.426
I didn't know it was higher than
the kids that I was playing with in my

00:25:51.426 --> 00:25:54.932
neighborhood in the summers, but
the ambient standard was very high.

00:25:54.932 --> 00:25:59.473
So when you took standardized tests,
you came across as being very prepared for

00:25:59.473 --> 00:26:00.370
those things.

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:05.110
So that closed one form
of opportunity gap.

00:26:05.110 --> 00:26:10.025
Also when you go into business, or
in my case, into the practice of law,

00:26:10.025 --> 00:26:15.420
the opportunity to work on the best
cases isn't distributed evenly.

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:18.650
This is the stock and trade of lawyers.

00:26:18.650 --> 00:26:23.510
In my case, I was very fortunate
that the then general counsel of

00:26:23.510 --> 00:26:27.900
Merck took an interest in me and
made sure that I got really good cases.

00:26:27.900 --> 00:26:33.080
And then I learned to try sophisticated
cases, and that made my career what it is.

00:26:33.080 --> 00:26:35.940
So one form of opportunity
gap was education.

00:26:35.940 --> 00:26:39.828
The other form of opportunity gap is,
once I arrived in the institution,

00:26:39.828 --> 00:26:42.852
who got the best opportunities
inside the institution?

00:26:42.852 --> 00:26:45.340
And then the third one is this access gap.

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:50.210
So the access gap is what I would
define as being, do people have access

00:26:50.210 --> 00:26:55.140
to the people in the firm who can make or
break their career?

00:26:55.140 --> 00:27:00.040
So I sit before you as the CEO of Merck,
and the reason I am the CEO of Merck

00:27:00.040 --> 00:27:05.060
is that Roy Vagelos, who was the CEO two
removed from me, took an interest in me.

00:27:06.370 --> 00:27:10.210
Hired me in the company as a lawyer,
called me into his office as soon as he

00:27:10.210 --> 00:27:13.730
got me to sign up, and said,
you cannot be a lawyer.

00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:15.860
I said to him, what do you mean?

00:27:15.860 --> 00:27:18.400
I'd like to contribute to
Merck in my own discipline.

00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:19.350
And his reply was,

00:27:19.350 --> 00:27:22.540
that's the most ridiculous thing
I've ever heard in my life.

00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:23.830
We're in the medicine business.

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:25.260
We're not in the legal business.

00:27:25.260 --> 00:27:27.613
And he forced me out of my comfort level.

00:27:27.613 --> 00:27:29.086
And as a result of that,

00:27:29.086 --> 00:27:34.070
I got access to working in broader areas
of the company as I sit here today.

00:27:34.070 --> 00:27:38.755
I realize that I'm the product of
being given access to those broader

00:27:38.755 --> 00:27:41.278
opportunities inside the company.

00:27:41.278 --> 00:27:43.562
So that Is an important issue.

00:27:43.562 --> 00:27:49.087
There was a New York Times front page
story, you might have seen it last fall,

00:27:49.087 --> 00:27:53.507
that said the majority of both men and
women are uncomfortable

00:27:53.507 --> 00:27:57.850
with close mentoring relationships
between men and women.

00:27:57.850 --> 00:27:59.361
Well if that's the case,

00:27:59.361 --> 00:28:03.757
then we're putting women in the position
where they lack the mentorship and

00:28:03.757 --> 00:28:07.619
the sponsorship that's necessary
to be successful in business.

00:28:07.619 --> 00:28:13.640
And so we have to mindful that
talent may be evenly distributed.

00:28:13.640 --> 00:28:17.710
I believe it is, but opportunities and
access are not evenly distributed.

00:28:19.760 --> 00:28:21.620
&gt;&gt; You are at the very top now,

00:28:21.620 --> 00:28:25.750
you have access to boardrooms that
none of us are able to sit in.

00:28:25.750 --> 00:28:28.670
Minority underrepresentation on boards has
received a lot of attention from the media

00:28:28.670 --> 00:28:30.130
in the last few years.

00:28:30.130 --> 00:28:34.410
From your perspective, from where you sit,
do you think attitudes and

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:37.064
perceptions in boards is really changing?

00:28:37.064 --> 00:28:40.861
&gt;&gt; So I have access to a limited
number of boards, of course,

00:28:40.861 --> 00:28:43.270
my board is extremely enlightened.

00:28:43.270 --> 00:28:46.592
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:28:46.592 --> 00:28:51.908
[APPLAUSE]

00:28:51.908 --> 00:28:53.255
&gt;&gt; Pete Wendell's in the front row.

00:28:53.255 --> 00:28:57.500
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; No, I do think that

00:28:58.590 --> 00:29:03.420
generally speaking,
the conversation is happening around

00:29:03.420 --> 00:29:09.120
board rooms about representation,
about diversity.

00:29:09.120 --> 00:29:11.640
I think that there still are challenges.

00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:17.160
So for example, there's a lot of focus
now on recruiting women to boards.

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:18.440
I'll use that as an example.

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:25.020
But the selection criteria
have lagged that conversation.

00:29:25.020 --> 00:29:29.240
So everybody's eager to recruit women to
boards, but they still say we want CEOs.

00:29:30.350 --> 00:29:34.868
Well, there are more CEOs with the first
name Michael than there are women CEOs in

00:29:34.868 --> 00:29:36.075
the Fortune 500.

00:29:36.075 --> 00:29:39.963
So if you're going to insist on women and
saying that you have to use

00:29:39.963 --> 00:29:44.790
the same traditional criteria, then
you're at odds with your actual intent.

00:29:44.790 --> 00:29:48.240
So, I think we have to open up our minds,

00:29:48.240 --> 00:29:51.960
because there's talent everywhere,
and I think we have to be open.

00:29:51.960 --> 00:29:54.570
But when you have that conversation,

00:29:54.570 --> 00:29:59.560
you have to guard against the reaction
that you're lowering standards now.

00:29:59.560 --> 00:30:03.330
We're bringing women into the board room,
but we've lowered the standards.

00:30:03.330 --> 00:30:11.307
And in fact, actually, I think the biggest
problem with boards is group think.

00:30:11.307 --> 00:30:14.888
And so, if you get a bunch of people
who come from the same background,

00:30:14.888 --> 00:30:17.742
they're more likely to see
the problem the same way than

00:30:17.742 --> 00:30:20.430
people who come from
different backgrounds.

00:30:20.430 --> 00:30:24.150
The value of my board,
frankly, is two things.

00:30:24.150 --> 00:30:28.980
They bring varied experiences that are
very different from the management teams,

00:30:28.980 --> 00:30:32.380
including an outside-in view of Merck.

00:30:32.380 --> 00:30:35.680
And the other one is that while
they're empathetic, they're just not

00:30:35.680 --> 00:30:39.080
too empathetic, so that they don't
let us get away with our bullshit.

00:30:39.080 --> 00:30:42.320
So at the end of the day,
you want people who come

00:30:42.320 --> 00:30:46.020
into the board room with very different
experiences and perspectives.

00:30:46.020 --> 00:30:48.230
That's how you get the best deliberations.

00:30:50.550 --> 00:30:53.845
&gt;&gt; You remain one of the very few
minority CEOs in the Fortune 500.

00:30:53.845 --> 00:30:57.816
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there are three of us,
out of 500.

00:30:57.816 --> 00:30:58.478
&gt;&gt; Where all have you been?

00:30:58.478 --> 00:31:01.304
&gt;&gt; There used to be six
of us in the happy days,

00:31:01.304 --> 00:31:04.220
the halcyon days of African American CEOs.

00:31:04.220 --> 00:31:07.120
A few years ago, there were a whole
six out of 500, now there are three.

00:31:09.050 --> 00:31:10.910
&gt;&gt; We're all hoping to be
leaders in the future.

00:31:10.910 --> 00:31:14.400
What do you want all of us to do to
make sure we change this for the better?

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:18.500
&gt;&gt; So I think this issue of diversity is
one of these issues that I've started off

00:31:18.500 --> 00:31:20.680
saying is a very challenging and

00:31:20.680 --> 00:31:26.500
nuanced issue, that it's really difficult
to discuss in corporate America.

00:31:26.500 --> 00:31:30.722
Because what happens in the conversation,
inevitably,

00:31:30.722 --> 00:31:35.470
is people say, in effect,
let me tell you what my experience is.

00:31:35.470 --> 00:31:38.090
And you're not allowed to
challenge my experience.

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:41.720
And that leads people to
retreat to different camps,

00:31:41.720 --> 00:31:45.220
to be defensive,
to engage in the rhetoric of blame,

00:31:45.220 --> 00:31:48.380
which doesn't solve anything,
as far as I'm concerned.

00:31:48.380 --> 00:31:52.736
So I would say that for
the young students who are here today,

00:31:52.736 --> 00:31:57.521
I hope that you actually understand
the points that I made about how

00:31:57.521 --> 00:32:01.643
opportunity and
access isn't distributed [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:01.643 --> 00:32:05.378
And I hope that you're willing to take
steps to ensure that we close those gaps,

00:32:05.378 --> 00:32:08.960
whether you're talking about What's
happening in public schools in our

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:13.360
society, whether you're talking about
what's happening in corporations.

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:17.050
I think enlightened leaders can
provide those opportunities.

00:32:17.050 --> 00:32:17.750
I'll say it for

00:32:17.750 --> 00:32:22.670
the last time, I am the product of
a CEO who looked at me and thought

00:32:22.670 --> 00:32:27.150
that I had much more potential than I ever
would've dreamed that I would of had.

00:32:27.150 --> 00:32:30.465
I hope I have done a good
job as the CEO of Murk but

00:32:30.465 --> 00:32:35.333
the opportunity came from somebody
who was willing to invest in me.

00:32:35.333 --> 00:32:37.474
&gt;&gt; So
again before we turn to audience Q&amp;A,

00:32:37.474 --> 00:32:40.969
I've got one last huge societal
problems for you to solve.for us.

00:32:40.969 --> 00:32:44.391
If you succeed at your job you
will save the employee lives,

00:32:44.391 --> 00:32:46.180
it's obviously a good thing.

00:32:46.180 --> 00:32:49.440
But there's an aging population and
the associated burden's worry

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:50.130
&gt;&gt; Yeah It does.

00:32:50.130 --> 00:32:56.210
I mean, we talk about the cost to
the health care system, of our drugs.

00:32:56.210 --> 00:32:58.490
So that's certainly a challenge
to the health care system.

00:32:58.490 --> 00:33:03.295
But the fundamental challenge in our
health care system is chronic disease.

00:33:03.295 --> 00:33:07.990
90% of the costs in the healthcare
system is from chronic disease and

00:33:07.990 --> 00:33:11.510
chronic disease is correlated with age.

00:33:11.510 --> 00:33:14.450
So the good news is with all
of these medical advances,

00:33:14.450 --> 00:33:18.480
people are living longer,
longevity is the good thing.

00:33:18.480 --> 00:33:23.090
But when it comes to healthcare
consumption, longevity isn't a good thing.

00:33:23.090 --> 00:33:28.098
The older people are,
the more healthcare services they need.

00:33:28.098 --> 00:33:32.097
And as a compassionate society I would
hope the more health care services we're

00:33:32.097 --> 00:33:33.270
willing to give them.

00:33:33.270 --> 00:33:37.050
But the aging of our society
is a major challenge for

00:33:37.050 --> 00:33:39.020
the economics of our health care system.

00:33:39.020 --> 00:33:43.350
In fact, some estimates are that a third
of all health care spending is in the last

00:33:43.350 --> 00:33:44.230
two months of life.

00:33:46.380 --> 00:33:50.750
We have to think about that and we have to
think about what's the compassionate and

00:33:50.750 --> 00:33:53.770
rational way to deal
with people as they age.

00:33:55.120 --> 00:33:58.570
&gt;&gt; So kind of a, half of us here are
second year MBAs, we're about to graduate.

00:33:58.570 --> 00:33:59.290
We've got a month to go.

00:33:59.290 --> 00:34:01.490
We're going to have as much
fun as possible in that month.

00:34:01.490 --> 00:34:03.505
But soon reality's going to
hit us in the face.

00:34:03.505 --> 00:34:07.252
So I'd like to give you the opportunity
to give us some quick words of wisdom

00:34:07.252 --> 00:34:10.240
before we go,
in the form of a lightning round.

00:34:10.240 --> 00:34:12.640
So I'm going to hit you with two options,
and

00:34:12.640 --> 00:34:15.500
then just say back the one
you think is most appealing.

00:34:15.500 --> 00:34:16.960
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; So should

00:34:16.960 --> 00:34:19.280
we take time finding the perfect job or
just get on the ladder?

00:34:20.320 --> 00:34:21.570
&gt;&gt; I say get on the ladder.

00:34:24.700 --> 00:34:26.107
&gt;&gt; Stay in the US or move to China?

00:34:26.107 --> 00:34:28.560
&gt;&gt; Stay in the US.
&gt;&gt; Go into tech or into finance?

00:34:28.560 --> 00:34:29.190
Go into tech.

00:34:30.480 --> 00:34:32.840
&gt;&gt; Communication,
should we be blunt or tactful?

00:34:32.840 --> 00:34:37.420
&gt;&gt; I think we should be tactful
at all times but honest.

00:34:37.420 --> 00:34:41.655
&gt;&gt; Is Bitcoin a bubble or the real deal?

00:34:41.655 --> 00:34:45.180
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well Bill Gates says it's a bubble,

00:34:45.180 --> 00:34:46.365
so who am I to argue?

00:34:46.365 --> 00:34:49.880
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Beyonce or Rihanna?

00:34:49.880 --> 00:34:50.410
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry?

00:34:50.410 --> 00:34:52.764
&gt;&gt; Beyonce or Rihanna?

00:34:52.764 --> 00:34:56.531
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Beyonce.

00:34:56.531 --> 00:35:02.220
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Personal philanthropy,

00:35:02.220 --> 00:35:04.880
should we be giving time now or
money later?

00:35:04.880 --> 00:35:09.645
&gt;&gt; I'd say both really,
but give money later.

00:35:09.645 --> 00:35:10.750
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now

00:35:10.750 --> 00:35:13.010
that's the question at
this gentlemen over here.

00:35:13.010 --> 00:35:15.780
If you have money you an make
a big difference in the world.

00:35:17.840 --> 00:35:19.450
&gt;&gt; East coast or West coast?

00:35:19.450 --> 00:35:20.495
&gt;&gt; Clearly East coast.

00:35:20.495 --> 00:35:23.970
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:35:23.970 --> 00:35:26.533
&gt;&gt; Especially Philadelphia, right?

00:35:26.533 --> 00:35:28.702
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Kids now or kids later?

00:35:28.702 --> 00:35:29.412
&gt;&gt; Kids later.

00:35:29.412 --> 00:35:34.651
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'll tell you I have two,

00:35:34.651 --> 00:35:37.556
it's an overrated experience when
they get to be here on this show.

00:35:37.556 --> 00:35:41.300
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Don't have to tell me.

00:35:41.300 --> 00:35:44.493
Given the state of the world today,
optimism or realism?

00:35:44.493 --> 00:35:46.430
&gt;&gt; I say optimism, for sure.

00:35:46.430 --> 00:35:51.210
&gt;&gt; And finally, the big one,
LeBron or Jordan?

00:35:51.210 --> 00:35:53.230
&gt;&gt; No question, Jordan.

00:35:53.230 --> 00:35:55.996
&gt;&gt; Yeah!
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:35:55.996 --> 00:35:58.190
&gt;&gt; Ladies and gentlemen, thanks so much.

00:35:58.190 --> 00:36:00.991
Please welcome him back, Ken Frasier.

00:36:00.991 --> 00:36:02.375
It's all in fun, thank you.

00:36:02.375 --> 00:36:07.485
[MUSIC]

